## Counting Words with Map

Counting frequency is what Maps do best. For each word, the key is the word, the value is how many times you've seen it.

### The Counting Pattern

```xiom
fn count_word(counts: Map[Str,Int], word: Str) {
  match counts.get(word) {
    Some(current) -> counts.insert(word, current + 1);
    None -> counts.insert(word, 1);
  }
}
```

This is the core algorithm: if the word already exists, add 1 to its count. If not, start at 1.

### Processing a Whole Sentence

```xiom
let words: Vec[Str] = Vec[Str].new();
words.push("the");
words.push("cat");
words.push("and");
words.push("the");
words.push("hat");

let counts: Map[Str,Int] = Map[Str,Int].new();
for word in words {
  count_word(counts, word);
}
```

After processing, the Map contains: "the" → 2, "cat" → 1, "and" → 1, "hat" → 1.

### Printing Results

```xiom
fn print_counts(counts: Map[Str,Int], word_list: Vec[Str]) {
  for word in word_list {
    match counts.get(word) {
      Some(n) -> io.println(word + ": " + string.int_to_str(n));
      None -> {}
    }
  }
}
```

### Full Program

```xiom
fn main() -> Int {
  let text: Vec[Str] = Vec[Str].new();
  text.push("hello");
  text.push("world");
  text.push("hello");

  let counts: Map[Str,Int] = Map[Str,Int].new();
  for word in text {
    match counts.get(word) {
      Some(n) -> counts.insert(word, n + 1);
      None -> counts.insert(word, 1);
    }
  }

  match counts.get("hello") {
    Some(n) -> io.println("hello: " + string.int_to_str(n));
    None -> {}
  }
  match counts.get("world") {
    Some(n) -> io.println("world: " + string.int_to_str(n));
    None -> {}
  }

  return 0;
}
```

